";}i:2;i:9065;}i:182;a:3:{i:0;s:11:"strong_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:9426;}i:183;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"6\.";}i:2;i:9428;}i:184;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"strong_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:9431;}i:185;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:460:" The UNIDROIT Principles offer a harmonized framework for handling digital assets in private transactions, including control, transfer, and security interests. Though not insolvency-specific, they can influence how these assets are treated in proceedings. The ambiguity around cryptocurrency's legal status poses a key challenge, with the G20's recent recommendations for regulation and oversight potentially impacting how these assets are handled globally.  
";}i:2;i:9433;}i:186;a:3:{i:0;s:11:"strong_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:9893;}i:187;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:3:"7\.";}i:2;i:9895;}i:188;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"strong_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:9898;}i:189;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:379:" While not explicitly addressing environmental trade-offs, the IBC mandates compliance with all applicable laws, implicitly encompassing environmental regulations. Future considerations include prioritizing environmental claims, assigning responsibility for compliance during proceedings, and balancing environmental obligations with business rescue and creditor satisfaction.  
